---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import PlantAdaptSim from '../../simulators/plantadapt/PlantAdaptSim.astro';
import { plantAdaptationsQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/plant-adaptations';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what', label: 'What is an adaptation?' },
  { id: 'kinds', label: 'Structural, physiological and behavioural' },
  { id: 'xerophytes', label: 'Xerophytes: life in dry places' },
  { id: 'hydrophytes', label: 'Hydrophytes: life in water' },
  { id: 'halophytes', label: 'Halophytes: life with salt' },
  { id: 'nutrients', label: 'Poor soils: carnivores and epiphytes' },
  { id: 'cold', label: 'Seasons and cold' },
  { id: 'compare', label: 'Comparing the strategies' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the model simplifies' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  { text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Sections 30.1–30.5: Plant Form and Physiology. OpenStax.', url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/30-introduction' },
  { text: 'Taiz, L., Zeiger, E., Møller, I. M., & Murphy, A. (2015). Plant Physiology and Development (6th ed.), chapters on water balance, abiotic stress and mineral nutrition. Sinauer Associates.' },
  { text: 'Lambers, H., & Oliveira, R. S. (2019). Plant Physiological Ecology (3rd ed.). Springer.' },
  { text: 'Ellison, A. M., & Adamec, L. (Eds.). (2018). Carnivorous Plants: Physiology, Ecology, and Evolution. Oxford University Press.' },
  { text: 'Tomlinson, P. B. (2016). The Botany of Mangroves (2nd ed.). Cambridge University Press.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="botany"
  topic="plant-adaptations"
  description="Interactive plant adaptations lab: grow cacti, water lilies, mangroves, Venus flytraps, bromeliads, oaks and sunflowers side by side, change temperature, water, salinity, light and nutrients, and inspect the adaptations that let each one survive."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={plantAdaptationsQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      A cactus can go months without rain. A water lily lives with its roots buried in airless mud. A mangrove drinks sea
      water, and a Venus flytrap eats insects. Plants cannot walk away from a harsh place, so their bodies and chemistry are
      shaped to suit it. Grow two plants side by side, change the conditions, and find out which adaptations keep each one
      alive.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <PlantAdaptSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li>Choose a <strong>habitat</strong> to set the conditions, or adjust the temperature, water, salinity, light and nutrients yourself.</li>
      <li><strong>Drag a plant</strong> from the tray into plot A or B (or use the menus). The simulation runs a day every half second; use <strong>Step 1 day</strong> to go one day at a time.</li>
      <li>Compare the two plants’ <strong>stress factors</strong>. The red outline marks the factor limiting each plant, and the graph records their health.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Inspect</strong> (or tap a plant) to explore its adaptations, then tap each numbered point.</li>
      <li>Try the classic tests: a cactus and a sunflower in the desert; a water lily in a dried-out pond; a flytrap with and without insects; an oak below 5 °C. You can also drag the fly onto a flytrap.</li>
      <li><strong>Reset</strong> returns to the desert with a cactus and a sunflower.</li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="what">What is an adaptation?</h2>
  <p>
    An <strong>adaptation</strong> is an inherited feature that helps an organism survive and reproduce in its environment.
    Adaptations arise through natural selection over many generations: individuals whose features suit the conditions leave
    more offspring. Because plants are rooted in one place, they must cope with whatever the place offers: drought or flood,
    salt, shade, frost or poor soil.
  </p>

  <h2 id="kinds">Structural, physiological and behavioural</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Plant examples</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Structural</strong>: physical features of the body</td><td>Spines, thick cuticles, air spaces, prop roots, trap leaves</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Physiological</strong>: internal chemical or life processes</td><td>CAM photosynthesis, salt exclusion, leaf fall, digestive enzymes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Behavioural</strong>: actions in response to the environment</td><td>Rare in plants; growth responses (tropisms) and a flytrap snapping shut are sometimes described this way</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Most plant adaptations are structural or physiological. Unlike animals, plants respond mainly by growing differently or changing their chemistry.</p>

  <h2 id="xerophytes">Xerophytes: life in dry places</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Xerophytes</strong> are adapted to dry environments such as deserts. Their adaptations either
    <strong>conserve water</strong> or <strong>get more of it</strong>. Cacti have leaves reduced to spines, a
    <strong>thick waxy cuticle</strong>, pleated stems with <strong>water-storage tissue</strong>, and wide, shallow roots
    that catch brief rain. Many use <strong>CAM photosynthesis</strong>, opening their stomata at night and keeping them
    closed in the heat of the day. Other xerophytes have rolled leaves, hairy leaves or sunken stomata, which trap a layer
    of moist air, or very deep roots. Not all desert plants work the same way: some, like desert annuals, avoid drought by
    surviving as seeds and growing only after rain.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hydrophytes">Hydrophytes: life in water</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Hydrophytes</strong> live in or on water. Water is plentiful, but oxygen is not: waterlogged mud contains very
    little. Water lilies have <strong>air spaces (aerenchyma)</strong> that carry oxygen down to their roots and help the
    leaves float, <strong>stomata on the upper surface</strong> of the floating leaves, and <strong>flexible stalks</strong>
    with little strengthening tissue, because the water supports them. Their roots and water-transporting tissue are
    reduced. Fully submerged plants, such as pondweeds, have thin, finely divided leaves that absorb gases straight from the
    water.
  </p>

  <h2 id="halophytes">Halophytes: life with salt</h2>
  <p>
    Salty soil makes it hard for roots to take in water by osmosis, and salt is toxic inside cells.
    <strong>Halophytes</strong> tolerate it. Red mangroves <strong>exclude most salt at their roots</strong>; other mangroves
    excrete salt through glands on their leaves or store it in leaves that are later shed. Mangroves also cope with
    tidal flooding: prop roots brace them in soft mud, and pores (lenticels) on aerial roots, or upright breathing roots
    called pneumatophores, take in air for roots buried in oxygen-poor mud.
  </p>

  <h2 id="nutrients">Poor soils: carnivores and epiphytes</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Carnivorous plants</strong> such as the Venus flytrap, pitcher plants and sundews live in sunny, wet, acidic bogs
    whose soil holds little nitrogen or phosphorus. They still make their food by <strong>photosynthesis</strong>; captured
    insects are digested by enzymes to supply <strong>extra nitrogen and phosphorus</strong>. A flytrap only closes if a
    trigger hair is touched twice within about 20 seconds, which avoids wasting a trap on a raindrop.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Epiphytes</strong> grow on other plants without feeding on them, unlike parasites. In the dim rainforest
    understorey, perching on a branch reaches more light. Tank bromeliads collect rainwater in a cup of overlapping leaves
    and absorb water and nutrients through scales on the leaves, while orchids absorb water with spongy roots.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cold">Seasons and cold</h2>
  <p>
    Temperate trees such as oaks face freezing winters, when water in the soil is locked up as ice. Deciduous trees
    <strong>drop their leaves</strong> in autumn, recovering nutrients first, and survive the winter
    <strong>dormant</strong>, with next year’s leaves protected in scaly buds. Evergreen conifers instead have narrow,
    waxy needles that lose little water.
  </p>

  <h2 id="compare">Comparing the strategies</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Main challenge</th><th>Key adaptations</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Xerophytes</td><td>Too little water</td><td>Spines, waxy cuticle, water storage, CAM, wide or deep roots</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hydrophytes</td><td>Too little oxygen for roots</td><td>Aerenchyma, upper-surface stomata, flexible stems</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Halophytes</td><td>Salt</td><td>Salt exclusion or excretion, succulent or waxy leaves</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Carnivorous plants</td><td>Few nutrients</td><td>Traps, digestive glands, trigger hairs</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Epiphytes</td><td>No soil, low light below</td><td>Perching habit, water tanks, absorbing scales or roots</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    An adaptation is only useful in the right place. The sunflower, with large leaves and open stomata, outgrows a cactus in
    moist, fertile soil but dies within days in the desert; a cactus rots in waterlogged soil.
  </p>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Agriculture</strong>: breeders develop drought- and salt-tolerant crops for dry or salty farmland, using genes from wild relatives.</li>
    <li><strong>Coastal protection</strong>: mangrove forests absorb wave energy and are replanted to protect shores from storms.</li>
    <li><strong>Gardening</strong>: xeriscaping uses drought-adapted plants to save water in dry regions.</li>
    <li><strong>Conservation</strong>: bog plants like the Venus flytrap, which grows wild only in a small area of the Carolinas, are threatened when wetlands are drained or fertilised.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>“Carnivorous plants get their food from insects.”</strong> They photosynthesise; prey provides mineral nutrients.</li>
    <li><strong>“Epiphytes are parasites.”</strong> They only use the host for support.</li>
    <li><strong>“Plants adapt during their lifetime when conditions change.”</strong> Adaptations are inherited and evolve over generations; an individual can only adjust within limits (for example, closing its stomata).</li>
    <li><strong>“All desert plants are cacti.”</strong> Different desert plants use different strategies, from deep roots to short life cycles.</li>
    <li><strong>“Water plants have no need for oxygen.”</strong> Their roots need oxygen too; air channels supply it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the model simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    Each plant’s tolerances are illustrative values based on its known biology, not measurements. The model tracks six
    stress factors on a 0–100% scale and assumes the most severe one limits health, a simplification of how stresses really
    combine; a shortage of nutrients is treated as stunting growth (it never drops below 35%) rather than killing quickly. Health is not the same as fitness: real survival also depends on reproduction, competition, pests, the plant’s
    age and how long a stress lasts. Water is modelled as a simple store that fills and empties, the soil is uniform, and
    each habitat is represented by a single set of average conditions. The fly is decorative except that switching insects
    on gives carnivorous plants their extra nutrients.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Plant adaptations are mainly structural or physiological, and are inherited.</li>
    <li>Xerophytes conserve and store water; hydrophytes supply their roots with oxygen; halophytes cope with salt.</li>
    <li>Carnivorous plants photosynthesise and catch prey for nitrogen and phosphorus; epiphytes perch on other plants without harming them.</li>
    <li>Deciduous trees survive cold winters by shedding their leaves and becoming dormant.</li>
    <li>An adaptation suits a particular environment; the same plant can thrive in one habitat and fail in another.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
